00:00:15,050 --> 00:00:28,940
Example 2: Compare 35.7 m and 35.698 m

3
00:00:30,420 --> 00:00:36,770
To compare two numbers, we do as follows:

4
00:00:40,450 --> 00:00:46,850
Compare whole parts of two decimal numbers.

5
00:00:49,970 --> 00:00:57,540
Compare decimal parts of two decimal numbers.

6
00:00:59,170 --> 00:01:20,970
We see two numbers 35.7 m and 35.698 m have equal whole parts (35 m), so we compare decimal parts.

7
00:01:23,080 --> 00:01:36,220
Decimal part of 35.7 m is (7 tenths) m = 7 dm = 700 mm

8
00:01:36,970 --> 00:01:59,310
Decimal part of number 35.698 m is 698 thousandths m = 698 mm

9
00:02:00,420 --> 00:02:19,940
Observe 700 mm > 698 mm (because 700 > 698, because at hundreds place 7 > 6)

10
00:02:21,340 --> 00:02:33,110
so 7 tenths m > 698 thousandths m.

11
00:02:35,140 --> 00:02:47,880
Therefore 35.7 m > 35.698 m

12
00:02:50,110 --> 00:03:19,340
Observe in comparison 35.7 > 35.698 (then whole part equal, but at tenths place 7 > 6)

13
00:03:23,220 --> 00:03:25,400
Conclusion:

14
00:03:26,540 --> 00:03:34,020
To compare two decimal numbers, we can do as follows:

15
00:03:35,910 --> 00:03:56,050
Compare whole parts of two numbers like comparing two natural numbers, decimal number whose whole part is greater is greater.

16
00:03:56,940 --> 00:04:18,140
If whole parts of two numbers are equal then compare decimal parts, successively from tenths place, hundredths place, thousandths place,…

17
00:04:18,450 --> 00:04:28,680
to same place, decimal number whose digit at corresponding place is greater is greater.

18
00:04:31,910 --> 00:04:43,800
If whole part and decimal part of two numbers are equal then two numbers are equal.

